WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.968 --> 00:00:01.667
-It's so great to see you.

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:02.667
-Great to see you!

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:04.334
-Thanks for being here
in person.

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:06.200
-This is exciting.
I haven't done a talk show

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:08.667
in person for years,
literally years.

00:00:08.667 --> 00:00:10.567
-It's been years.
-Yeah, so, it's great.

00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:13.000
It's interesting, we're playing
the True Confessions game

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:15.234
and then we're telling lies
and all this stuff

00:00:15.234 --> 00:00:17.801
and I just happened to mention
that you once fought

00:00:17.801 --> 00:00:20.067
Boom Boom Mancini in the ring
and --

00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:21.567
-I didn't fight him.
-Well...

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:24.801
-We sparred for a second.
-That counts, dude.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:24.801 --> 00:00:26.434
We went to commercial
and Questlove goes,

00:00:26.434 --> 00:00:28.567
"C'mere for a second."
He goes, "Did he fight?

00:00:28.567 --> 00:00:30.234
Did you just say he fought
Boom Boom? Back up, wait."

00:00:30.234 --> 00:00:33.167
[ Laughter ]
Explain. This is real?

00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:35.167
-There was this gym
in Santa Monica

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:39.200
that a friend of mine had
and it was actually --

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:40.367
I don't know
if I'm allowed to say

00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:41.501
it was owned by Bob Dylan
-Yeah, you can say.

00:00:41.501 --> 00:00:43.801
-and it was
underneath a synagogue.

00:00:43.801 --> 00:00:45.200
[ Laughter ]
-So, Bob Dylan --

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.501
No, this is a true story.
-It was a boxing gym

00:00:46.501 --> 00:00:49.434
underneath a synagogue and a lot
of actors went there.

00:00:49.434 --> 00:00:51.567
David Duchovny went there
and Pete Berg

00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:54.667
and Garry Shandling.
-Garry Shandling.

00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:55.701
-And --

00:00:55.701 --> 00:00:57.601
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:57.601 --> 00:00:59.133
Who was like a really good
boxer, by the way.

00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:02.033
-Did you ever see like
anyone fight Bob Dylan?

00:01:02.033 --> 00:01:04.868
[ Laughter ]
-I think I feel like Garry
got in the ring with Dylan.

00:01:04.868 --> 00:01:06.067
-Garry Shandling
fought Bob Dylan?

00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:07.767
[ Laughter ]
-I think he did.

00:01:07.767 --> 00:01:08.968
-This should've been the story.

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:10.868
-No, this is a movie.

00:01:10.868 --> 00:01:12.467
-It's insane.
Yeah, it's crazy.

00:01:12.467 --> 00:01:14.567
It was a great place.
-So, did you go in thinking

00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:16.367
that you would fight Bob Dylan?
-No, no, no, no.

00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:18.200
[ Laughter ]
I went in being afraid

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.534
that I'd run into Bob Dylan.
-Yeah. Just nervous?
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.534 --> 00:01:21.834
-Yeah, it's Bob Dylan.

00:01:21.834 --> 00:01:23.534
I passed him in the hall once
and I just sort of

00:01:23.534 --> 00:01:25.000
kept my head down.
-Yeah, of course.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.300
And then you get in the ring.
-Yeah, get in the ring.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.634
-You're hoping to fight
a comedian, maybe.

00:01:28.634 --> 00:01:30.601
-Yeah. Right, right, exactly.

00:01:30.601 --> 00:01:32.234
And Boom Boom Mancini was there.

00:01:32.234 --> 00:01:34.167
[ Laughter ]
And he goes, "Man, come on,
get up in the ring,"

00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:35.434
and so we like went a little bit

00:01:35.434 --> 00:01:38.334
and, you know,
he was really very nice

00:01:38.334 --> 00:01:39.534
and he didn't give me
any head shots.

00:01:39.534 --> 00:01:41.067
Because, you know,
I'm not a boxer, you know.

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:42.701
I do it to like work out.
-Of course.

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:44.133
-But, you know,
the second you get like

00:01:44.133 --> 00:01:46.801
a little tap in the nose,

00:01:46.801 --> 00:01:49.601
I just like, want to cry
and go to my mommy, go home.

00:01:49.601 --> 00:01:50.934
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah, exactly.

00:01:50.934 --> 00:01:52.767
You go, "No, I'm a comedian.
Please, I don't do this.

00:01:52.767 --> 00:01:54.367
I'm just doing this for fun,
a workout."

00:01:54.367 --> 00:01:56.701
-Yeah, but it was --
Yeah, it was an amazing --

00:01:56.701 --> 00:01:58.167
I mean, an amazing place.

00:01:58.167 --> 00:01:59.534
-I see you go
to the Knick game.

00:01:59.534 --> 00:02:01.767
I see you at the Knick games.
-Yes. Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:01.767 --> 00:02:03.534
-Did you like the Olympics?

00:02:03.534 --> 00:02:05.200
-I do. You know,
I do like the Olympics.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.901
I have a little bit
of a, you know,

00:02:06.901 --> 00:02:09.367
I resist the Olympics every time
they come around a little bit

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:11.968
just because it's so much.
It's such a big deal.

00:02:11.968 --> 00:02:15.300
And I think, when they started
to change the schedule,

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:16.667
you know,
it used to be every four years

00:02:16.667 --> 00:02:18.834
back in like the '70s and the
'80s, it was every four

00:02:18.834 --> 00:02:21.267
and then, at some point,
it became every two years.

00:02:21.267 --> 00:02:22.667
Like they were --
they started to --

00:02:22.667 --> 00:02:23.901
and then it got me confused

00:02:23.901 --> 00:02:25.634
because it felt like
the Olympics --

00:02:25.634 --> 00:02:27.267
-Like a "Real Housewives"
or something.

00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:28.901
You go, "One ends,
another begins."

00:02:28.901 --> 00:02:30.467
[ Laughter ]
-It was always either the
Olympics were about to begin

00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:33.434
or about to end and it took me
10 years to figure out

00:02:33.434 --> 00:02:34.901
that they were every two years.
-Yeah.

00:02:34.901 --> 00:02:36.167
-And then, yeah,
and then it just sort of like,

00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:37.868
it's always like so like,
I feel like

00:02:37.868 --> 00:02:38.834
I'm going to just live my life.

00:02:38.834 --> 00:02:40.601
I'm not going to get into this

00:02:40.601 --> 00:02:42.067
because I got too much going on.

00:02:42.067 --> 00:02:45.334
[ Laughter ]
And then, of course,
it's just ubiquitous

00:02:45.334 --> 00:02:48.367
and, you know,
inevitably, I get pulled in

00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:50.734
because I'm like sitting in bed
at 11:30 at night

00:02:50.734 --> 00:02:53.133
and, you know, all of a sudden,
I'm there

00:02:53.133 --> 00:02:54.767
and, you know, in 15 minutes,

00:02:54.767 --> 00:02:58.601
I'm just like obsessed
with men's slopestyle snowboard.

00:02:58.601 --> 00:03:02.567
[ Laughter ]
And I have this deep
institutional knowledge

00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:04.334
and I'm emotionally involved.

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:06.033
-You have no idea
what you're talking about.
-Yes.

00:03:06.033 --> 00:03:08.200
And my my wife will come in
and say like, "What's going on?"

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:09.868
I'm like, "Oh, my God!

00:03:09.868 --> 00:03:13.067
Red Gerard just nailed
his 1620 switch backside."

00:03:13.067 --> 00:03:15.000
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.234
-Red Gerard!
-Yeah.

00:03:16.234 --> 00:03:17.667
-Yes!
-And then, the judge --

00:03:17.667 --> 00:03:20.367
[ Applause ]

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:22.267
And then the judge --

00:03:22.267 --> 00:03:24.167
McMorris does it again,
he does the same trick,

00:03:24.167 --> 00:03:26.601
and the judge gives McMorris
a higher score!

00:03:26.601 --> 00:03:28.334
[ Laughter ]
When is the IOC going
to clamp down

00:03:28.334 --> 00:03:31.467
on International Ski Federation
and police the judging protocol

00:03:31.467 --> 00:03:33.300
so we can have fair
and balanced judging?

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.634
-That's exactly what I'm --

00:03:34.634 --> 00:03:37.634
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:03:37.634 --> 00:03:40.000
-You know what I'm saying?
-Finally!

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.167
-But, 20 minutes earlier,

00:03:41.167 --> 00:03:42.534
I didn't know what
Red Gerard was.

00:03:42.534 --> 00:03:43.834
[ Laughter ]

00:03:43.834 --> 00:03:46.801
It could've been a Ben &amp; Jerry's
like flavor or something.

00:03:46.801 --> 00:03:48.968
And now I'm ready to go to the
International Olympic Committee

00:03:48.968 --> 00:03:50.167
and fight for him.
-Fight for Red Gerard.

00:03:50.167 --> 00:03:54.801
-Yes. I got really deeply
into women's monobob.

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:56.667
-I didn't know that was a thing
until after the Super Bowl.

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:59.734
They're like, "And we're back
with women's monobob."

00:03:59.734 --> 00:04:03.000
I go, I don't even know --
-It's like a single bobsled

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:04.868
and two incredible stories.
We double medaled.

00:04:04.868 --> 00:04:07.334
The U.S. double medaled.
We had the silver and the gold.

00:04:07.334 --> 00:04:10.901
Incredible stories.
One, the silver medalist,

00:04:10.901 --> 00:04:14.033
I think, Elana Meyers Taylor?
-My gosh, I feel like your wife.

00:04:14.033 --> 00:04:16.167
I feel like your wife right now.
Oh, my God.

00:04:16.167 --> 00:04:18.701
[ Laughter ]
-But she has an incredible story
because she got COVID

00:04:18.701 --> 00:04:20.167
and she was quarantining
in her room

00:04:20.167 --> 00:04:23.434
for like seven or eight days
before and training in her room

00:04:23.434 --> 00:04:25.467
and then like came
out of her room like Superwoman

00:04:25.467 --> 00:04:27.501
and medaled.
And then the other,

00:04:27.501 --> 00:04:29.667
the gold medalist,
she has an amazing story.

00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:33.367
She defected from Canada
to come here and compete.

00:04:33.367 --> 00:04:36.100
She literally left her own
country to -- Yes.

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:37.601
-'Cause she's like, "I want to
represent the U.S."

00:04:37.601 --> 00:04:38.634
-Yeah.

00:04:38.634 --> 00:04:40.267
-But I watched the Olympics
and I go,

00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:44.200
"This is great to watch because
I would never do any of these."

00:04:44.200 --> 00:04:45.934
-No.
-Maybe curling.

00:04:45.934 --> 00:04:47.133
-Yeah, well, curling --

00:04:47.133 --> 00:04:49.200
it feels like
my brother-in-law could do it.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.100
-But that's how easy
they make it look, right?

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:52.234
They go, "Oh, I can do it."

00:04:52.234 --> 00:04:54.200
-But they are -- I mean,
it's almost like a joke,

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.534
because people talk
about curling like a joke.

00:04:55.534 --> 00:04:56.534
But I don't understand.

00:04:56.534 --> 00:04:57.934
They have -- they have brooms.

00:04:57.934 --> 00:05:00.067
-They've come on the
show and they've shown me that.

00:05:00.067 --> 00:05:01.801
It's very difficult.
-Yeah.

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:03.033
-'Cause once you get
the speed going,

00:05:03.033 --> 00:05:04.834
that's one thing,
but then it's up to the team.

00:05:04.834 --> 00:05:06.167
You've got to trust these people

00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:09.267
to sweep the speed up
or slow it down or not sweep.

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:13.767
-It's to sweep or not sweep.
That is the question.

00:05:13.767 --> 00:05:17.334
But I watched that monobob,
and I go, "No way."

00:05:17.334 --> 00:05:18.467
No, I would never
do anything like that.

00:05:18.467 --> 00:05:20.200
-Oh, no, it looks --
It's insanely scary.

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:21.367
-And they've got these, like --

00:05:21.367 --> 00:05:23.334
these shoes that have
these teeth on them

00:05:23.334 --> 00:05:24.334
that dig into the ice.

00:05:24.334 --> 00:05:25.367
-I didn't even do that.

00:05:25.367 --> 00:05:26.667
I wear ice skates
that are, like,

00:05:26.667 --> 00:05:28.000
rubber ice skates
if I go ice skating.

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:31.200
-Yeah. I just get
stressed out on ice.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:33.133
-But you are a sporty guy.
-I do like sports.

00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:34.267
Yeah, I love tennis.

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:37.534
I'm a really big tennis --
-Do you play?

00:05:37.534 --> 00:05:41.400
-I play a little bit.
But I'm a huge Rafa Nadal fan.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:42.801
He just won the Australian Open.
-He's unbelievable.

00:05:42.801 --> 00:05:43.901
-He's just had
the most incredible --

00:05:43.901 --> 00:05:46.033
-Nadal is unbelievable.

00:05:46.033 --> 00:05:48.801
-He just had the most incredible
comeback of all time

00:05:48.801 --> 00:05:50.133
at the Australian open.

00:05:50.133 --> 00:05:54.567
Amazing. And I got to know him
a little bit about --

00:05:54.567 --> 00:05:56.300
I don't know --
about seven or eight years ago

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.601
'cause we were at a tournament
and we ran into his people.

00:05:58.601 --> 00:06:01.067
Then we got introduced.
So we sort of stayed in touch.

00:06:01.067 --> 00:06:03.133
And he invited me,
a few years back, to see

00:06:03.133 --> 00:06:05.567
an exhibition match at
Madison Square Garden,

00:06:05.567 --> 00:06:06.567
which was really exciting,
because it was --

00:06:06.567 --> 00:06:07.901
-Wow.

00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:09.801
-Yeah, because, you know,
first of all, it's the Garden,

00:06:09.801 --> 00:06:11.767
so it's, you know,
that electric atmosphere.

00:06:11.767 --> 00:06:13.567
And you don't see
tennis there that often.

00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:15.200
And we got to sit
in the front row.

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.868
And it was him and
Juan Martín del Potro,

00:06:16.868 --> 00:06:19.567
who is another amazing
Argentinean player.

00:06:19.567 --> 00:06:21.367
And they're doing the match,

00:06:21.367 --> 00:06:23.167
and about halfway through
the match,

00:06:23.167 --> 00:06:25.133
Nadal stops and he goes, "Wait.

00:06:25.133 --> 00:06:27.501
Should we make it doubles?"

00:06:27.501 --> 00:06:30.167
That's my Nadal impression.
-That's not bad.

00:06:30.167 --> 00:06:33.100
-It's not very good.
And then he turns --

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:34.367
-I think that's the
first time anyone's done

00:06:34.367 --> 00:06:35.567
a Rafa Nadal --

00:06:35.567 --> 00:06:37.434
-Yeah, he's --
Yeah, and he says, "Doubles?"

00:06:37.434 --> 00:06:38.634
And then he looks at me
and he goes,

00:06:38.634 --> 00:06:40.734
"Come on, Ben Stiller,
Ben Stiller, come on."

00:06:40.734 --> 00:06:42.434
And he comes up to me, yeah,

00:06:42.434 --> 00:06:44.367
and he pulls me
out of the crowd,

00:06:44.367 --> 00:06:47.067
so, which is simultaneously,
like, the greatest --

00:06:47.067 --> 00:06:48.801
Yeah, I'm having, like --
It's like a dream come true

00:06:48.801 --> 00:06:50.000
to be in Madison Square Garden.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:51.701
And people start cheering.

00:06:51.701 --> 00:06:55.701
And, also, it's this nightmare
that's happening simultaneously,

00:06:55.701 --> 00:06:58.067
because I realize
I really don't play tennis.

00:06:58.067 --> 00:06:59.334
-Yeah, I don't want to do this
in front of everybody.

00:06:59.334 --> 00:07:01.100
-And I'm dressed in
street clothes.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:02.667
You know, usually,
I wear tennis shoes.

00:07:02.667 --> 00:07:03.934
Ironically,
I'm wearing shoe-boots,

00:07:03.934 --> 00:07:05.234
like, leather shoe-boots.

00:07:05.234 --> 00:07:08.701
So -- And I got up there,
and then del Potro pulls --

00:07:08.701 --> 00:07:09.767
He goes, "Wait a minute.

00:07:09.767 --> 00:07:11.400
I must have a partner."

00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:14.968
And he pulls an 8-year-old girl
out of the crowd.

00:07:14.968 --> 00:07:18.167
Like, the cutest little
8-year-old girl.

00:07:18.167 --> 00:07:20.234
And, you know -- And,
immediately, I just died inside,

00:07:20.234 --> 00:07:23.033
because I knew there was no way
to get out of this thing alive,

00:07:23.033 --> 00:07:25.734
because if I hit a winner on
the 8-year-old girl,

00:07:25.734 --> 00:07:28.701
everybody, you know, hates me,
and then I don't have

00:07:28.701 --> 00:07:30.834
enough skills to sort of,
like, you know, hold it back,

00:07:30.834 --> 00:07:32.801
because just,
like, I'm all or nothing.

00:07:32.801 --> 00:07:36.067
And, so...
[ Laughter ]

00:07:36.067 --> 00:07:39.734
So, you know, Nadal serves it.

00:07:39.734 --> 00:07:42.834
The 8-year-old girl hits it back
perfectly across the net.

00:07:42.834 --> 00:07:44.501
The crowd cheers -- "Ahh!"

00:07:44.501 --> 00:07:47.634
Comes to me,
boom, right into the net.

00:07:47.634 --> 00:07:48.834
Immediately, just, like --

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:52.133
Just all of
Madison Square Garden, "Ugh!"

00:07:53.934 --> 00:07:56.968
And, basically,
we play 4 more points.

00:07:56.968 --> 00:07:59.267
Every time, the girl hits it.
They cheer every time.

00:07:59.267 --> 00:08:01.868
I went back into
the net four times.

00:08:01.868 --> 00:08:05.667
And del Potro puts the girl
on his shoulders

00:08:05.667 --> 00:08:07.400
and they're cheering
and she walks off.

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:09.467
And then I walk --
I didn't make eye contact with

00:08:09.467 --> 00:08:12.300
Nadal, my wife, anybody.

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:14.667
-Anybody in the crowd.

00:08:14.667 --> 00:08:16.701
-But the good thing that came
out of this was that

00:08:16.701 --> 00:08:20.667
I resolved to become the
best tennis player in the world.

00:08:20.667 --> 00:08:21.667
-And you're going to be.

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:23.067
-Someday, I will.
-Yeah.

00:08:23.067 --> 00:08:24.467
-I started taking
lessons about --

00:08:24.467 --> 00:08:26.934
It was like six years ago,
and I started taking lessons.

00:08:26.934 --> 00:08:30.467
And so I'm ready
if it ever happens, like,

00:08:30.467 --> 00:08:32.868
in life that somebody
pulls me out of the crowd

00:08:32.868 --> 00:08:33.901
to go play tennis with them,

00:08:33.901 --> 00:08:35.100
which probably
won't ever happen again.

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:36.400
-Yeah.

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:40.534
-And the little girl --
I want a rematch with her.

00:08:40.534 --> 00:08:42.267
-Well, I will say --
-'Cause I do feel --

00:08:42.267 --> 00:08:44.234
[ Applause ]
Thank you. Yeah.

00:08:44.234 --> 00:08:47.400
I feel like -- I do feel
like she was a ringer.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.300
I feel like, yeah,
she had abilities beyond her --

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:50.467
what she seemed to --

00:08:50.467 --> 00:08:52.100
-Yeah, and now you know what
you're doing.

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:53.834
-Yes, now I'm ready.
Now I'm ready.

00:08:53.834 --> 00:08:55.133
